VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Change in useful life: compute revised depreciation prospectively
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Dalton Manufacturing purchased equipment for $480,000 with a $30,000 salvage value and a 10-year useful life on January 1, Year 1, using straight-line depreciation. At the beginning of Year 5, the company revises the remaining useful life to 4 years (from the original 6 remaining) with no change in salvage value. What is the depreciation expense for Year 5?', '["$45,000", "$56,250", "$67,500", "$75,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-17, a change in estimated useful life is a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively. The book value at the beginning of Year 5 is $300,000 ($480,000 minus $180,000 accumulated over 4 years at $45,000). Subtracting the $30,000 salvage value gives $270,000 depreciable base, spread over the revised 4-year remaining life, yielding $67,500. Option D ($75,000) incorrectly ignores the salvage value in the revised calculation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Where cumulative effect adjustment is recorded under retrospective application
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, where is the cumulative effect of a change in accounting principle recorded when retrospective application is used?', '["As an adjustment to the opening balance of retained earnings of the earliest period presented", "As a separate line item on the current-year income statement", "As a component of other comprehensive income", "As a deferred charge on the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 250-10-45-5 requires that the cumulative effect of a change in accounting principle be reflected by adjusting the opening balance of retained earnings for the earliest period presented. Unlike the former cumulative-effect method eliminated by SFAS 154, the adjustment never flows through the current income statement. Option B reflects the superseded approach under APB 20, which is no longer valid under current GAAP.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Non-counterbalancing error definition
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, what is a non-counterbalancing error?', '["An error that is automatically corrected by adjusting entries", "An error that does not self-correct over two successive accounting periods", "An error that affects only the balance sheet and not the income statement", "An error that involves a simultaneous change in accounting principle"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-20, a non-counterbalancing error is one whose effect on the financial statements does not reverse naturally within two accounting periods. Examples include understating the cost of a depreciable asset, which causes depreciation to be misstated over the entire asset life. Option A describes a counterbalancing error, which self-corrects within two periods (such as an inventory misstatement).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Definition of impracticable under ASC 250
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, retrospective application of a change in accounting principle is considered impracticable when which condition exists?', '["The entity prefers to apply the change prospectively", "The change would reduce reported earnings in prior periods", "The entity cannot obtain auditor approval for restatement", "The entity cannot determine the period-specific effects after making every reasonable effort"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 250-10-20 defines retrospective application as impracticable when the entity cannot determine the effects on prior periods after making every reasonable effort. This might occur when the data needed to apply the change retroactively was not collected in prior periods. Option A is incorrect because an entity''s preference does not override the retrospective application requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Compute restated net income after error correction with tax
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Prescott Corp. reported net income of $500,000 in Year 1. In Year 2, the company discovers it failed to record $80,000 of rent expense in Year 1. The Year 1 financial statements have already been issued. Assuming a 25% tax rate, what is the restated net income for Year 1?', '["$420,000", "$440,000", "$460,000", "$500,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23, error corrections are reported through restatement of prior-period financial statements. The omitted $80,000 rent expense reduces pre-tax income, but the associated $20,000 tax benefit ($80,000 times 25%) partially offsets the effect. Restated net income is $500,000 minus $60,000 = $440,000. Option A ($420,000) incorrectly applies the full $80,000 without considering the tax benefit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Classify change in bad debt estimation technique
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Meridian Services previously estimated its allowance for doubtful accounts using an aging schedule and switches to a percentage-of-sales method. Under ASC 250, how should Meridian classify and report this change?', '["Change in accounting principle applied retrospectively", "Error correction reported through restatement", "Change in accounting estimate applied prospectively in current and future periods", "Change in reporting entity requiring restatement of all prior periods"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-20, a change in the technique used to estimate the allowance for doubtful accounts is a change in accounting estimate. The underlying accounting principle (recognizing bad debt expense through an allowance) remains the same; only the estimation methodology differs. Estimate changes are applied prospectively per ASC 250-10-45-17. Option A is incorrect because no change in accounting principle has occurred.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Prior period adjustment restricted to error corrections
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, the term "prior period adjustment" refers exclusively to which type of accounting event?', '["Correction of an error in previously issued financial statements", "Voluntary change in accounting principle", "Change in accounting estimate with a material effect", "Adoption of a new accounting standard"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 250-10-45-23 restricts the use of prior period adjustments to the correction of errors in previously issued financial statements. Changes in accounting principle are handled through retrospective application, and estimate changes are applied prospectively. Option B is incorrect because principle changes adjust beginning retained earnings through retrospective application, a distinct mechanism from a prior period adjustment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — LIFO to FIFO change: retrospective application required
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'On January 1, Year 3, Orion Manufacturing voluntarily changes its inventory valuation method from LIFO to FIFO. The cumulative difference between LIFO and FIFO inventory at December 31, Year 2, is $150,000 (FIFO higher). Orion presents two years of comparative statements. Under ASC 250, how should Orion report this change?', '["Recognize a $150,000 gain on the Year 3 income statement", "Disclose the change in the notes without adjusting any prior amounts", "Apply the change only to inventory purchased in Year 3 and forward", "Retrospectively apply FIFO to all prior periods presented and adjust beginning retained earnings of the earliest period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-5, a voluntary change from LIFO to FIFO is a change in accounting principle that requires retrospective application. Orion must restate all prior periods presented as if FIFO had always been used and adjust the opening retained earnings of the earliest period for the cumulative effect. Option A reflects the superseded cumulative-effect method under APB 20.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Mandatory ASU adoption classified as change in principle
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Glacier Corp. adopts a newly issued ASU that changes the recognition criteria for revenue from technology contracts. The ASU requires modified retrospective transition. Under ASC 250, how should Glacier classify this adoption?', '["Change in accounting estimate because it involves judgment", "Change in accounting principle required by a new standard", "Error correction because the prior method is now deemed incorrect", "Change in reporting entity due to altered revenue streams"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1, the adoption of a newly issued standard that changes recognition, measurement, or classification criteria is a change in accounting principle. When the new standard specifies its own transition method (modified retrospective), the entity follows that guidance per ASC 250-10-45-3. Option C is incorrect because the prior method was correct under the standards in effect at the time; a new standard does not retroactively make the prior method an error.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Non-counterbalancing error: effect persists on retained earnings
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'In Year 1, Franklin Corp. recorded the $200,000 purchase of a machine with a 10-year useful life as repairs expense instead of capitalizing it. If the error is not discovered until Year 5, what is the effect on Year 5 beginning retained earnings (ignoring taxes, no salvage value, straight-line depreciation)?', '["Understated by $120,000", "Understated by $200,000", "Understated by $80,000", "Correctly stated because the error self-corrected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is a non-counterbalancing error under ASC 250 because the effect persists beyond two periods. In Year 1, expensing the $200,000 asset overstated expenses by $200,000, but the missing depreciation ($20,000/year times 4 years = $80,000) partially offsets the error. The net understatement of retained earnings at Year 5 is $200,000 minus $80,000 = $120,000. Option D is incorrect because only counterbalancing errors self-correct within two periods.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Interim period accounting change: retrospective restatement of interim periods
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Summit Industries makes a voluntary change in accounting principle effective at the beginning of its third quarter (July 1). The company presents quarterly interim financial data. Under ASC 250-10-45, how must Summit report the change in its interim financial statements?', '["Apply the change only to the third and fourth quarters of the current year", "Disclose the change in the third-quarter notes only with no restatement", "Retrospectively apply the change to all previously reported interim periods of the current and prior fiscal years presented", "Defer the change to the beginning of the next fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-7, when a change in accounting principle is made in an interim period, the entity must apply the change retrospectively to all prior interim periods presented, including those of the current fiscal year. Financial statements for pre-change interim periods are restated as if the new principle had been in effect from the beginning. Option A incorrectly limits the change to remaining quarters, ignoring retrospective application.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Error correction effect on earnings per share
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Cascade Technologies restates its Year 1 financial statements to correct a material error that increases Year 1 net income by $300,000. Under ASC 250, what is the effect on the Year 1 earnings per share previously reported in comparative financial statements?', '["EPS is unaffected because it is a per-share metric independent of restatement", "EPS is restated only if the error exceeds 5% of net income", "EPS is disclosed in the notes but not changed on the face of the statements", "EPS for Year 1 must be restated to reflect the corrected net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23 and ASC 260-10-45-13, when prior-period financial statements are restated for an error correction, all per-share amounts reported for those periods must also be restated. EPS is an integral part of the income statement and must be recalculated using the corrected net income. Option A is incorrect because restating net income changes the EPS numerator, requiring updated per-share amounts.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Change in salvage value: compute revised annual depreciation
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Hartwell Corp. purchased a delivery truck for $120,000 on January 1, Year 1, with an estimated salvage value of $12,000 and a 9-year useful life (straight-line). At the beginning of Year 4, Hartwell revises the salvage value to zero with no change in remaining useful life. What is the depreciation expense for Year 4?', '["$12,000", "$14,000", "$13,333", "$20,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-17, a change in salvage value is a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively. At the beginning of Year 4, the book value is $84,000 ($120,000 minus $36,000 accumulated at $12,000/year for 3 years). With revised salvage of zero and 6 remaining years, new depreciation is $84,000 divided by 6 = $14,000. Option A ($12,000) incorrectly continues the original calculation. Option D ($20,000) incorrectly uses original cost rather than current book value.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Indirect effects of change in principle: profit-sharing not applied retrospectively
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Axiom Corp. changes its inventory method from LIFO to FIFO on January 1, Year 3. The company has a nondiscretionary profit-sharing plan paying employees 10% of pre-tax income. Retrospective restatement increases Year 2 pre-tax income by $200,000 and Year 1 pre-tax income by $150,000. Under ASC 250, how should Axiom account for the additional profit-sharing obligations created by the restated higher income?', '["Recognize the additional profit-sharing expense in Year 3 as an indirect effect of the accounting change", "Include the additional profit-sharing in the retrospective restatement of Years 1 and 2", "Adjust beginning retained earnings for the indirect effects but exclude them from comparative income statements", "Ignore the profit-sharing impact entirely because indirect effects receive no accounting recognition"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 250-10-45-8 prohibits adjusting prior-period financial statements for indirect effects of a change in accounting principle. Indirect effects, such as changes to nondiscretionary profit-sharing contributions based on restated income, are recognized in the period the accounting change is made (Year 3), not retroactively. Option B is incorrect because only direct effects (the inventory valuation itself) are applied retrospectively. The entity must also disclose indirect effects per ASC 250-10-50.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Cumulative effect placement in comparative statements
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Crestview Inc. voluntarily changes an accounting principle on January 1, Year 5. The company presents three years of comparative income statements (Years 2, 3, and 4). The cumulative effect of the change at January 1, Year 2, is $90,000. Under ASC 250, where is the $90,000 cumulative effect reported?', '["On the Year 5 income statement as a below-the-line item", "As a footnote disclosure only with no financial statement adjustment", "As an adjustment to the opening retained earnings balance of Year 2", "Allocated proportionally across Years 2, 3, and 4"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 250-10-45-5 requires that the cumulative effect for periods before the earliest period presented be reflected as an adjustment to opening retained earnings for that earliest period. Since Year 2 is the earliest period presented, the $90,000 cumulative effect adjusts Year 2 opening retained earnings. Option D is incorrect because each year is restated individually, and only the pre-presentation cumulative amount goes to opening retained earnings.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Percentage-of-completion estimate revision: prospective treatment
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Bridgeport Construction uses the percentage-of-completion method for a 3-year project. At the end of Year 2, the company revises total estimated costs from $5 million to $6 million. Under ASC 250, how should Bridgeport classify and account for this revision?', '["Change in accounting principle requiring retrospective restatement of Year 1", "Error correction requiring restatement of Year 1 financial statements", "Change in reporting entity requiring restatement of all prior periods", "Change in accounting estimate applied prospectively by adjusting Year 2 and Year 3 computations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-17, a revision of total estimated contract costs is a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively. The company adjusts the percentage-of-completion calculation in Year 2 and future periods without restating Year 1. The cumulative catch-up approach recognizes the revised estimate''s effect in the current period. Option A is incorrect because the accounting method has not changed; only the estimated inputs have been updated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Multiple simultaneous errors: evaluate combined effect on net income
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Apex Corp. discovers two errors in its Year 1 financial statements (already issued): (1) ending inventory was overstated by $40,000, and (2) depreciation expense was understated by $25,000. Both errors affect only Year 1. Ignoring taxes, evaluate the combined effect of these errors on Year 1 net income as originally reported.', '["Net income was overstated by $15,000", "Net income was overstated by $65,000", "Net income was understated by $65,000", "Net income was overstated by $40,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23, each error is evaluated individually and then the net effect is determined. The $40,000 inventory overstatement caused COGS to be understated, overstating net income by $40,000. The $25,000 depreciation understatement further overstated net income. Both errors inflated income in the same direction, so the combined overstatement is $65,000. Option A ($15,000) incorrectly nets the errors as if they offset each other.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Error correction journal entry for overstated revenue with tax effect
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'In Year 3, Pinnacle Services discovers that it recognized $60,000 of revenue in Year 2 for services not yet performed (Year 2 is presented comparatively). The Year 2 tax expense associated with this revenue was $15,000. What is the correcting entry when Pinnacle restates Year 2?', '["Debit retained earnings $45,000, debit deferred tax asset $15,000, and credit unearned revenue $60,000", "Debit retained earnings $60,000 and credit unearned revenue $60,000", "Debit revenue $60,000 and credit cash $60,000", "Debit retained earnings $45,000 and credit revenue $45,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23, the restatement reverses the prematurely recognized revenue by establishing a $60,000 unearned revenue liability. The associated $15,000 tax benefit (from reducing taxable income) creates a deferred tax asset, reducing the net retained earnings impact to $45,000. Option B ($60,000 to retained earnings) ignores the income tax effect, overstating the retained earnings adjustment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: hard / L3 — Modified retrospective transition: cumulative effect at adoption date only
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Evergreen Corp. adopts a new ASU effective January 1, Year 4. The ASU permits full retrospective or modified retrospective transition. Evergreen elects modified retrospective. The cumulative effect at January 1, Year 4, is a $180,000 increase to net assets. Under ASC 250, which statement correctly describes the modified retrospective approach?', '["Restate all comparative periods and adjust the earliest period''s opening retained earnings", "Record the $180,000 as a gain on the Year 4 income statement", "Recognize the $180,000 cumulative effect as an adjustment to January 1, Year 4, retained earnings without restating prior periods", "Spread the $180,000 adjustment evenly across Year 4 quarterly reports"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-5, modified retrospective application recognizes the cumulative effect as an adjustment to opening retained earnings at the adoption date (January 1, Year 4) without restating comparative periods. Option A describes full retrospective application. Option B incorrectly treats the cumulative effect as income, which was eliminated by SFAS 154. Modified retrospective is common in recent ASUs such as ASC 842 and ASC 606.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Required disclosures for a change in accounting estimate
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, what must an entity disclose when a change in accounting estimate has a material effect on the financial statements?', '["The auditor''s opinion on the reasonableness of the new estimate", "The nature of the change and its effect on income from continuing operations, net income, and related per-share amounts", "The indirect effects of the estimate change on future cash flows", "A reconciliation of the old and new estimation methodologies"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 250-10-50-4 requires disclosure of the nature of a material estimate change and its effect on income from continuing operations, net income, and related per-share amounts for the current period. If the change also affects future periods, that fact must be disclosed when estimable. Option D is not required; the entity explains the nature and financial statement impact, not a detailed methodological reconciliation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — SEC SAB 99/108 qualitative materiality for error restatement
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Phoenix Corp. (a public company) discovers an error that overstated net income by $50,000 in Year 2, when net income was $2,500,000 (a 2% error). However, the error eliminated what would have been a year-over-year earnings decline: without the error, Year 2 income would have been $2,450,000 versus Year 1 income of $2,480,000. Under SEC SAB 99 and SAB 108, should Phoenix restate?', '["No, because the 2% quantitative threshold is below the typical 5% benchmark", "No, because only quantitative analysis determines materiality under GAAP", "Yes, only if the external auditor issues a qualified opinion", "Yes, because the error masks a trend reversal, which is a qualitative materiality factor under SAB 99"]'::jsonb, 3, 'SAB 99 (codified in ASC 250-10-S99) states that exclusive reliance on quantitative benchmarks is inappropriate for assessing materiality. Even if a misstatement is quantitatively small (2%), qualitative factors such as masking a change in earnings trend or meeting analyst expectations can render it material. Here, the $50,000 error concealed a year-over-year decline, specifically cited in SAB 99 as a qualitative indicator. Option A incorrectly relies solely on a quantitative threshold.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Change in reporting entity: comparative statements retrospectively adjusted
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, how are comparative financial statements presented when there is a change in reporting entity?', '["All prior-period financial statements presented are retrospectively adjusted to show information for the new reporting entity", "Only the current period reflects the new reporting entity structure", "A pro forma column is presented alongside the historical statements", "Prior periods are presented as originally reported with a footnote describing the change"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 250-10-45-21 requires that when a change in reporting entity occurs, all prior-period financial statements presented for comparative purposes must be retrospectively adjusted to reflect the new reporting entity. This ensures comparability across periods. Option D is incorrect because simply adding a footnote without adjusting prior periods impairs comparability.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Loss of significant influence: equity method cessation applied prospectively
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Navarro Inc. holds a 30% interest in Sterling Corp. and applies the equity method. On July 1, Year 4, Sterling issues additional shares to outside investors, diluting Navarro''s interest to 15%. Navarro no longer has significant influence. Under ASC 250, how should Navarro account for this change?', '["Retrospectively restate all prior periods to remove equity method income", "Continue applying the equity method until the investment is sold", "Prospectively cease applying the equity method and carry the investment at its equity-method book value as of the change date", "Write the investment down to zero and recognize a loss"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 323-10-35-36 and ASC 250-10-45-17, when an investor loses significant influence due to dilution, it ceases applying the equity method prospectively. The investment retains its equity-method carrying amount at the date significant influence is lost and is subsequently accounted for under ASC 321. Option A is incorrect because this results from changed circumstances, not a voluntary change in accounting principle.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Scope of ASC 250: three categories of accounting changes
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Which of the following types of accounting changes falls within the scope of ASC 250?', '["Changes caused by adopting a new depreciation software system", "Changes resulting from initial adoption of GAAP by a first-time preparer", "Changes in payroll processing procedures", "Changes in accounting principle, accounting estimate, and corrections of errors"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 250-10-15-1 establishes the scope to include changes in accounting principle, changes in accounting estimate, and corrections of errors in previously issued financial statements. It also addresses changes in reporting entity. Option B is outside the scope because ASC 250 applies to changes from one acceptable method to another, not first-time GAAP adoption. Options A and C involve operational procedures, not accounting measurements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Compute after-tax cumulative effect adjustment to earliest-period opening RE
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'On January 1, Year 4, Redstone Corp. changes an accounting principle and presents comparative statements for Years 2 through 4. The pre-tax effects of the change are: Year 1 and prior, $100,000 increase; Year 2, $40,000 increase; Year 3, $30,000 increase. The tax rate is 25%. What is the after-tax cumulative effect adjustment to Year 2 opening retained earnings?', '["$75,000", "$100,000", "$127,500", "$170,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-5, only the cumulative effect for periods before the earliest period presented is recorded as an adjustment to opening retained earnings. Since Year 2 is the earliest period, only the $100,000 from Year 1 and prior applies. After the 25% tax effect, the adjustment is $75,000 ($100,000 times 0.75). Option C ($127,500) incorrectly includes the Year 2 and Year 3 effects, but those years are restated individually in the comparative statements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Error in depreciation useful life: correcting entry for prior years
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'In Year 3, Magnus Corp. discovers that equipment purchased for $300,000 at the beginning of Year 1 was incorrectly assigned a 15-year useful life instead of the correct 10-year life (no salvage value, straight-line). The Year 3 books have not yet been closed. Ignoring taxes, what correcting entry should Magnus record for the Years 1 and 2 understatement?', '["Debit depreciation expense $20,000 and credit accumulated depreciation $20,000", "Debit retained earnings $20,000 and credit accumulated depreciation $20,000", "Debit retained earnings $30,000 and credit accumulated depreciation $30,000", "Debit depreciation expense $10,000 and credit accumulated depreciation $10,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23, the correction of a prior-period error adjusts retained earnings, not current-period expense. Correct depreciation is $30,000/year ($300,000 divided by 10); recorded was $20,000/year ($300,000 divided by 15). The $10,000/year shortfall for Years 1-2 totals $20,000. The entry debits retained earnings and credits accumulated depreciation for $20,000. Option A incorrectly runs the prior-period correction through current-year depreciation expense.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Restatement vs retrospective application: terminology distinction
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Under ASC 250, the term "restatement" is specifically reserved for which type of accounting event?', '["A voluntary change in accounting principle", "A mandatory change required by a new ASU", "The correction of an error in previously issued financial statements", "A change in accounting estimate with material effect"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 250-10-20 defines restatement as the process of revising previously issued financial statements to correct an error. The term "retrospective application" is used for changes in accounting principle, while "prospective application" describes estimate changes. Option A is incorrect because principle changes use retrospective application, not restatement. This terminology distinction is important for proper classification and disclosure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Counterbalancing error in second year before closing: correcting entry required
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'On December 15, Year 2, before closing the books, Keystone Corp. discovers that ending inventory at December 31, Year 1, was understated by $50,000. Year 1 books are closed but Year 2 books are still open. Ignoring taxes, evaluate whether and how Keystone should correct this counterbalancing error.', '["No entry is needed because the error will self-correct when Year 2 closes", "Debit inventory $50,000 and credit retained earnings $50,000", "Debit retained earnings $50,000 and credit cost of goods sold $50,000", "Debit cost of goods sold $50,000 and credit retained earnings $50,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-23, errors must be corrected when discovered regardless of whether they are counterbalancing. Because Year 2 books are open, both the opening retained earnings understatement ($50,000 from Year 1) and the Year 2 COGS understatement ($50,000 from understated beginning inventory) must be corrected. The entry debits COGS and credits retained earnings for $50,000. Option A is incorrect; while the error would self-correct after Year 2 closes, leaving it uncorrected misstates Year 2 net income.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — LIFO-to-FIFO change creates deferred tax liability for book-tax difference
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'Templeton Corp. changes from LIFO to FIFO on January 1, Year 3, using retrospective application. The LIFO reserve (the excess of FIFO inventory over LIFO inventory) was $260,000 at December 31, Year 2. The company continues using LIFO for tax purposes. Assuming a 25% tax rate, what deferred tax liability should appear on the restated December 31, Year 2, balance sheet related to the inventory method change?', '["$65,000", "$195,000", "$260,000", "No deferred tax liability is required"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 740-10-25-20 and ASC 250-10-45-5, when retrospective application changes book inventory from LIFO to FIFO while LIFO is retained for tax, a taxable temporary difference arises equal to the LIFO reserve. The deferred tax liability at December 31, Year 2, is $260,000 times 25% = $65,000. Option B ($195,000) confuses the after-tax retained earnings adjustment with the DTL amount. Option D is incorrect because the book-tax difference creates an obligation for future taxes.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Restatement labeling requirements in comparative statements
(2, 'Accounting Changes and Error Corrections', 'When Prism Corp. restates its Year 1 financial statements for a material error correction and presents them alongside Year 2 statements, under ASC 250 how should the restated Year 1 columns be labeled?', '["No special labeling is required for restated prior-period columns", "The Year 1 columns must be labeled as ''As Restated''", "The Year 1 columns must include the word ''Unaudited''", "The restated amounts are shown only in the footnotes, not on the face of the statements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 250-10-45-23 and ASC 250-10-50-7 require that when prior-period financial statements are restated for error corrections, the affected columns must be clearly labeled as ''As Restated'' to alert users that the amounts differ from those originally reported. Option A is incorrect because failing to label restated amounts could mislead users who might assume the figures match the originally issued statements.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
